// Standard questions, ccao. Original items written against the published exam guide. Built from content/en by tools/build-en.js.
window.QUESTIONS = window.QUESTIONS || {};
window.QUESTIONS.ccao = [
// ---- Domain 1: Prompting and Task Execution (14%) ----
{ id:'ccao-1-01', d:1, o:0, q:"A sales associate asks Claude only to “write next quarter's proposal” and gets generic, unusable text. What is the best thing to fix first?",
  c:["Spell out the task context and the deliverable: client, problem, goal of the proposal, length and tone", "Switch to a more capable model and send the same “write next quarter's proposal” request once more", "Add only a role to the top of the request, such as “Act as a professional sales representative”", "Send the same request three times to get three proposals, then use whichever one looks best"],
  a:[0], e:"The basis of an effective prompt is saying who it is for, what it is for, and what shape it should take. Resending or changing models cannot supply information Claude does not have. A role can help, but it does not fill in missing context." },
{ id:'ccao-1-02', d:1, o:0, q:"You are making a technical request: write usage steps from an internal API specification. Which way of prompting is most likely to improve accuracy?",
  c:["Attach the relevant part of the spec and name the reader (non-engineer operators) and the level of detail", "Ask Claude to “write it clearly, politely and in as much detail as possible so anyone can follow”", "Skip the spec, give only the API name and company name, and let Claude write from what it knows", "Keep the request as short as possible to give Claude freedom, then fix whatever comes back"],
  a:[0], e:"Claude writes more accurately the more source material it has in hand. An internal API spec is not in its training data, so without the attachment the steps are guesses (hallucination). Naming the reader and level of detail shapes the output." },
{ id:'ccao-1-03', d:1, o:0, q:"Which TWO are real effects of including examples in a prompt?",
  c:["They convey the format and tone you want more precisely than a description in words", "They show Claude how it should decide in ambiguous cases", "Claude copies example content straight into its output, so examples should be avoided", "Adding examples raises the limit on attachments and context that Claude can process"],
  a:[0,1], e:"Few-shot examples are the most reliable way to convey format, tone and decision criteria. Verbatim copying mostly happens with a single, narrow example; two or three varied examples generalize. Examples do not change the context limit." },
{ id:'ccao-1-04', d:1, o:1, q:"You want Claude to “research pricing, features and reputation for five competitors, build a comparison table, and summarize our options.” What is the best way to proceed?",
  c:["Put the research, the table and the options into one prompt and have Claude produce the finished piece in one go", "Split it into stages (organize each company, build the table, weigh options) and check each output first", "Ask for the options first to set a direction, then have Claude add supporting evidence for each company later", "Run the five companies in five separate chats at once to save time, then merge the results yourself"],
  a:[1], e:"Task decomposition splits a complex request into stages you can verify, with a person checking each output before it feeds the next. Asking for everything at once lets an early error travel to the end, and you cannot tell where it went wrong." },
{ id:'ccao-1-05', d:1, o:1, q:"From a long internal policy (80 pages) you need to “pull out only the remote-work clauses and summarize them.” What is a suitable first stage?",
  c:["Attach all 80 pages, ask right away to “summarize remote work,” and use the output as it is", "Ask first for extraction (the relevant clauses with page numbers), check it, then ask for the summary", "Have the whole policy summarized first, then look through that summary yourself for remote-work parts", "Ask Claude to “tell me only the important parts of this policy” and wait for remote work to come up"],
  a:[1], e:"Separating extraction from summarizing lets you verify gaps and errors by page number, which makes the later summary more trustworthy. Summarizing everything first is wasteful for this goal and risks dropping the remote-work clauses." },
{ id:'ccao-1-06', d:1, o:1, q:"When passing each stage's output to the next stage of a decomposed task, what matters most for keeping quality?",
  c:["Pasting the previous output straight into the next prompt is enough; no human check is needed", "A person checks each stage's output and corrects errors before it becomes the next input", "Use a different model for each stage so that every stage is handled by the best specialist", "Add as many fine-grained stages as possible so that each one handles the least information"],
  a:[1], e:"The value of decomposition is that you can check along the way. Passing outputs on unchecked wastes most of that value. Use as many stages as the task needs; more is not automatically better." },
{ id:'ccao-1-07', d:1, o:2, q:"Claude's first output is “too long, and the key points are buried.” What is the most efficient way to improve it?",
  c:["Write a completely different request from scratch and start over again in a brand-new conversation", "In the same conversation, give a specific edit: “cut to three key points, under 30 words each”", "In the same conversation, ask to “make it better and easier to read” a few times and see what happens", "Switch to another model, redo the same request from the start, and use whichever version is shorter"],
  a:[1], e:"Iterative refinement keeps the conversation context and tells Claude exactly what to change and how. “Make it better” does not say what should change." },
{ id:'ccao-1-08', d:1, o:2, q:"You keep refining a prompt but the output stays inconsistent. What should you do first to isolate the cause?",
  c:["Show one example of the output you want and say where yours differs", "Look up the temperature setting and change it to a lower value", "Report it to support as a Claude malfunction and wait for a reply", "Continue the conversation for ten more turns and see if it settles"],
  a:[0], e:"Showing the gap between what you expect and what you got lets Claude see the difference. Temperature is not adjustable in normal claude.ai use. Most inconsistency comes from ambiguous instructions." },
{ id:'ccao-1-09', d:1, o:2, q:"An associate adds “forget everything from our earlier conversation” every time they ask for an improvement. What is the problem with this habit?",
  c:["Nothing. A clean slate each time avoids being anchored by earlier output and gives higher quality", "The context built up in the conversation (sources, intent of edits) is thrown away, so improvements do not accumulate", "Claude cannot follow this instruction, so the output is exactly the same whether you write it or not", "It lowers token use and speeds things up, so adding it every time is actually the recommended practice"],
  a:[1], e:"Iterative refinement depends on accumulated context. Starting a new conversation is right when the topic changes, but discarding context while polishing the same deliverable sends you backwards." },
{ id:'ccao-1-10', d:1, o:3, q:"You structure prompts differently for brainstorming and for analysis. Which instruction suits brainstorming?",
  c:["“Show your evidence and state the single most defensible conclusion.”", "“Quantity over quality: 20 distinct ideas. No evaluating yet.”", "“Calculate using only the figures provided and include no guesses.”", "“No bullet points; lead with the conclusion in under 100 words.”"],
  a:[1], e:"Divergent work (brainstorming) goes best when evaluation is postponed and variety is requested. Convergent work (analysis, conclusions) needs strong evidence requirements and constraints. The objective is to change strategy by task type." },
{ id:'ccao-1-11', d:1, o:3, q:"Which instruction is especially worth adding when you give Claude a research task?",
  c:["Cite sources and dates, and mark anything you are not sure of as “uncertain”", "Use a polite register and wording that will not offend the reader", "State the conclusion first and list the reasons as bullets after it", "Write as long and detailed as you can and include related topics too"],
  a:[0], e:"In research, stating sources, dates and confidence is what makes verification possible. The other options are format choices with little effect on research accuracy." },
{ id:'ccao-1-12', d:1, o:3, q:"Which prompt element helps both when you ask for an email draft and when you ask for data analysis?",
  c:["State the purpose, the reader (or user) and the constraints (length, format, information to leave out)", "Always make the request in English and take the output in English before translating it yourself", "Ask for everything at once without stages and let Claude decide the whole structure in a single pass", "Open with “You are the world's leading expert” to draw out output that sounds like a real specialist"],
  a:[0], e:"Whatever the task type, stating purpose, reader and constraints helps. A role statement alone does not raise accuracy." },
{ id:'ccao-1-13', d:1, o:0, q:"When is it especially useful to specify in the prompt what the output must NOT include?",
  c:["For an external document that must not contain unreleased internal or personal information", "When you are chatting casually with Claude and want a relaxed, natural conversation", "When you want shorter output but would rather not decide yourself what should be cut", "When the headings you need are fixed and you want them written in that exact order"],
  a:[0], e:"Negative constraints (information or tone to leave out) bear directly on the safety of external documents. For shorter output set a length; for required elements use a positive instruction." },

// ---- Domain 2: Output Evaluation and Validation (21%) ----
{ id:'ccao-2-01', d:2, o:0, q:"You are evaluating a “quarterly sales analysis report” Claude produced. What is the best way to check accuracy and completeness?",
  c:["Check the report's figures against the source data and confirm every requested angle (by region, by product) is covered", "Read it to judge whether the prose is fluent and persuasive and whether executives would find the logic convincing", "Ask Claude “Is this report accurate and complete?” and treat it as finished if Claude says there are no problems", "Send the same request again to get a second report and treat it as correct if both reach the same conclusion"],
  a:[0], e:"Evaluate on two axes: checking against source data, and coverage of what was requested. Fluency is not a measure of accuracy. Self-assessment or matching regenerations may simply repeat the same error." },
{ id:'ccao-2-02', d:2, o:0, q:"Claude's output “covers only four of the five items requested.” How is this problem classified?",
  c:["Hallucination (fabricated facts)", "A lack of completeness (coverage)", "A bias (skew) problem", "A format (layout) error"],
  a:[1], e:"Not covering the requested scope is a completeness problem. Hallucination is making up things that are not true; bias is skew. Evaluate accuracy and completeness separately." },
{ id:'ccao-2-03', d:2, o:0, q:"Your team wants to standardize how outputs are evaluated. Which practice is most effective?",
  c:["Agree on check items such as accuracy, completeness, audience fit and format, and keep a record of what was verified", "Leave evaluation to each person's experience and instinct, and raise concerns in team meetings only when they come up", "Use Claude's output as it is by default, fix problems when they appear, and share those cases with the team afterward", "Have the manager read and approve every output, and align the whole team's evaluation on the manager's judgment"],
  a:[0], e:"Fixed criteria plus an evidence trail make evaluation quality less dependent on the individual. Manager approval of everything is impractical, and relying on instinct or after-the-fact fixes is risky." },
{ id:'ccao-2-04', d:2, o:1, q:"Claude states a figure with a specific source: “According to a 2024 survey (source: XYZ Research Institute)…” What is the right stance toward this statement?",
  c:["The source name and survey year are specific, so it can be trusted and used without checking", "Both the source and the figure may be fabricated, so go to the original material and confirm", "Checking the figure against the original is enough; the source name itself needs no checking", "Ask Claude “Does this source really exist?” and use the statement if Claude says that it does"],
  a:[1], e:"Producing plausible sources and figures is the classic hallucination. Being specific is not evidence of being right, and asking Claude to confirm itself is not reliable either." },
{ id:'ccao-2-05', d:2, o:1, q:"In a long conversation, Claude “recommends option A” early on and later says “option B is best.” What is the best response?",
  c:["Adopt the later answer, since it is a conclusion that takes more of the information into account", "Point out the inconsistency, state the evaluation criteria and have both reassessed; re-supply sources if needed", "Adopt the earlier answer, since it was a calmer judgment made before the conversation grew long", "Delete the chat, start over in a new conversation and adopt whatever answer comes out the first time"],
  a:[1], e:"Inconsistency comes from vague criteria or drift in context. The sound move is to make the criteria explicit and have the options reassessed. Order alone does not decide which is right." },
{ id:'ccao-2-06', d:2, o:1, q:"You asked Claude to summarize job candidates and notice it tends to emphasize one particular university. What is this a sign of, and what should you do?",
  c:["A sign of bias. Instruct it to assess only against the job requirements, and have a person review the output", "An accuracy problem. Regenerating with the same request will remove the emphasis on that university", "Not a problem. Education is important for comparing candidates, so emphasizing it is acceptable", "A format problem. Laying the items out in a table will make the emphasis much less noticeable"],
  a:[0], e:"Giving undue weight to a particular attribute is bias. In a high-risk area like hiring, state the evaluation criteria explicitly and always include human review." },
{ id:'ccao-2-07', d:2, o:2, q:"Before distributing Claude's “key points of the law change” inside the company, which fact-checking procedure is appropriate?",
  c:["Check article numbers, effective dates and figures one by one against the official text and primary sources", "Ask a different AI the same question and judge it correct if the two answers agree with each other", "If Claude's answer lists a “source,” the comparison against the original text can safely be skipped", "Distribute it first, then correct and redistribute any part that readers point out as being wrong"],
  a:[0], e:"Fact-checking means comparing against primary sources. Agreement between AIs is not independent verification. Corrections after distribution damage trust." },
{ id:'ccao-2-08', d:2, o:2, q:"To lower the cost of verification while keeping accuracy, what is effective to ask Claude for from the start?",
  c:["For each claim, note where in the attached material (page, heading) the support comes from", "Write in language that is as assertive as possible and avoid any hedged or tentative phrasing", "Leave out source notes to keep things short, so reading takes less time and checking is faster", "People will do the verifying, so ask Claude only for the output format and add nothing more"],
  a:[0], e:"When support is located for each claim, a person only needs to check those spots, which speeds verification. An assertive style makes verification harder." },
{ id:'ccao-2-09', d:2, o:2, q:"You had Claude do an analysis involving calculations (percentages, totals). What is the most reliable way to verify the results?",
  c:["Total the source data in a spreadsheet and compare with Claude's figures", "Ask Claude to “recheck the math” and accept it if the same figures return", "If the figures come out as round numbers, assume the math is correct", "Judge the figures by how persuasive and logical the written analysis is"],
  a:[0], e:"Recalculate numbers by an independent means such as a spreadsheet. Claude re-checking itself can repeat the same mistake." },
{ id:'ccao-2-10', d:2, o:3, q:"Which TWO of the following should be judged as “human review required” before Claude's output is used?",
  c:["A notice to a customer about changed contract terms", "A draft announcement for an internal team lunch", "A summary of a medication guide for medical institutions", "Brainstorming notes written only for your own use"],
  a:[0,2], e:"Human verification is required when the legal, financial or health impact is large, or when the output goes outside or into a specialist field. A light internal notice or personal notes have little impact, so the check can be lighter." },
{ id:'ccao-2-11', d:2, o:3, q:"What is the most rational basis for deciding whether human review is needed?",
  c:["The length of the output and how much jargon it contains", "How large the impact of an error would be, and whether it can be undone", "How confident the style of Claude's writing appears to be", "The time that passed between sending the request and getting output"],
  a:[1], e:"Whether review is needed is decided by risk: size of impact and reversibility. How confident the text sounds is not a measure of accuracy." },
{ id:'ccao-2-12', d:2, o:3, q:"Claude says, “I'm not certain about this point.” What should the associate do?",
  c:["Use the stated uncertainty as a cue: research that part yourself or confirm with a specialist", "Output that admits uncertainty cannot be trusted, so discard the whole answer and find another method", "Instruct it to “answer confidently and assertively” and have it rewrite the point as a firm statement", "Ignore that one sentence and treat the point as solid information in the same way as all the rest"],
  a:[0], e:"A statement of uncertainty is a useful signal for prioritizing verification. Forcing assertiveness invites unsupported assertions (hallucination)." },
{ id:'ccao-2-13', d:2, o:4, q:"An analysis written for the executive meeting should also go to frontline staff. What is an appropriate way to proceed?",
  c:["Hand out the executive document as it is and let people look up any terms they do not understand", "Have it rewritten for the readers (less jargon, actions up front) and have a person confirm accuracy", "Frontline staff matter less here, so have Claude make only a summary of the executive document", "Train frontline staff in executive terminology so they can read the same document, then hand it out"],
  a:[1], e:"Editing and adapting for the audience is one of the objectives. It must come with a check that the rewrite has not changed the facts." },
{ id:'ccao-2-14', d:2, o:4, q:"You have Claude produce two drafts for the same request and want to compare and choose. Which method works well?",
  c:["Pick the longer one, because more content means more of the reasoning is visible and more evidence is given", "Compare against criteria set in advance (goal met, accuracy, audience fit) and combine the best parts", "Pick the one produced first, because the later draft was influenced by the earlier one and is not independent", "Have Claude decide “which draft is better” with reasons, and adopt that judgment exactly as it is given"],
  a:[1], e:"Comparison is meaningful only when criteria are set first. Claude's own pick can be a reference, but the final decision is made by a person against the criteria." },
{ id:'ccao-2-15', d:2, o:5, q:"You will have Claude write an internal procedure, edit it many times and distribute it. Which output format fits best?",
  c:["Take it as an inline chat reply and search the conversation whenever you need it", "Create it as an Artifact, editing across versions and exporting when ready", "Have it output as JSON and import that into another tool to shape the procedure", "Have it read aloud and distribute the recording for staff to listen to"],
  a:[1], e:"Artifacts let you edit and update a deliverable (document, code, diagram) in its own space, which suits documents you revise repeatedly. Short answers fit inline; machine processing calls for structured data." },
{ id:'ccao-2-16', d:2, o:5, q:"From 300 customer survey responses you want to extract “request category, count, representative example” and load the result into another tool. Which output format is appropriate?",
  c:["A prose summary describing the trends in what customers asked for", "Structured data such as CSV or JSON", "A bulleted set of impressions listing typical opinions", "A report of several pages with background and analysis"],
  a:[1], e:"If another tool will process it, use structured data. Organizing and curating information and choosing the format is one of the objectives." },
{ id:'ccao-2-17', d:2, o:5, q:"You want to turn a long answer from Claude into a one-page handout for a meeting. Which instruction is appropriate for an associate?",
  c:["“Fit one page, structured as conclusion, three supporting points, next actions” (structure and length specified)", "“This is for a meeting, so please summarize the key points briefly and clearly,” then use what comes back", "Pick out the paragraphs that look important yourself, copy them, and trim until they fit on one page", "Tell Claude the meeting's purpose and attendees and have it produce a summary along with a proposed agenda"],
  a:[0], e:"Curating and organizing information goes as intended when you state the structure and the length." },
{ id:'ccao-2-18', d:2, o:1, q:"Claude's answer adds “supplementary information” that is not in the material you attached. What is the best response?",
  c:["The supplement is useful and helps in understanding the material, so keep it in the answer and use it", "Identify what is not in the material, then verify or delete it; from now on say “only from the attachment”", "If the supplement and the material disagree, assume the material is older and is the one in error", "Claude's training data is broad and accurate, so supplements outside the material need no checking"],
  a:[1], e:"Additions from outside the material may be right, but they are unverified. Limit the scope by instruction and separate out anything that did not come from the material." },

// ---- Domain 3: Product and Model Selection (12%) ----
{ id:'ccao-3-01', d:3, o:0, q:"You want to produce a weekly “competitor news roundup” over and over with the same instructions and reference material. Which claude.ai feature fits best?",
  c:["Open a new chat each time and paste the same instructions and reference material before asking", "Put the instructions and material in a Project and chat inside it", "Write the instructions and material in an Artifact and copy from it every week", "Use only Research and send the same search instructions each week for a report"],
  a:[1], e:"A Project holds custom instructions and knowledge (files) so you can chat repeatedly in the same context. It suits recurring routine work." },
{ id:'ccao-3-02', d:3, o:0, q:"On “whether to enter a new market,” you want a report backed by evidence drawn across many sources. Which feature fits best?",
  c:["Repeat one-off questions in normal chat, gather the sources yourself and assemble a report", "Have Research produce a cited investigation report, and have a person check the citations", "Have Artifacts create market charts and tables, and assemble your decision material from those", "Switch the model to Haiku, ask many quick questions and compile the answers into a report"],
  a:[1], e:"Research is meant for investigating many sources autonomously and reporting with citations. Checking the citations remains a human job." },
{ id:'ccao-3-03', d:3, o:0, q:"Which combination correctly describes when to use chat, Projects and Artifacts in claude.ai?",
  c:["Chat = one-off questions and drafts / Projects = workspace holding instructions and files / Artifacts = deliverables you develop", "Chat = a place to store files / Projects = deliverables you edit / Artifacts = one-off questions and quick drafts", "Chat = an ongoing workspace / Projects = one-off questions / Artifacts = where instructions and files are kept", "Chat = a feature for engineers / Projects = a feature for managers / Artifacts = a feature meant for everyone"],
  a:[0], e:"Choose by purpose. Selecting product features is the core of Domain 3." },
{ id:'ccao-3-04', d:3, o:1, q:"Which statement about the Claude model names Haiku, Sonnet and Opus is correct?",
  c:["Haiku is the lightest, fastest and cheapest; Opus is the most capable, for deep reasoning; Sonnet is the balance between", "Opus is the lightest, fastest and cheapest; Haiku is the most capable, for deep reasoning; Sonnet is the balance between", "All three perform the same; the only differences are naming by subscription plan and the cap on how often you can use them", "Haiku is for Japanese only, Opus is for English only, and Sonnet is the multilingual model that handles both languages"],
  a:[0], e:"The model families are separated by trade-offs in capability, speed and cost. Choose according to the task." },
{ id:'ccao-3-05', d:3, o:1, q:"You are asking for “the issues in a complex contract, with contradictions pointed out.” In general, which way of choosing a model fits best?",
  c:["Put speed first and use the lightest model; a person reads the issues and fills in what is missing", "It needs deep reasoning, so use the top model (or a deeper thinking setting); a person verifies the result", "Results are the same on every model, so use the cheapest one and make up for it by asking more often", "The model is irrelevant; a short prompt with only the contract attached will give a good enough result"],
  a:[1], e:"Complex reasoning is where the most capable models do best. Even they make mistakes, so verification is still needed." },
{ id:'ccao-3-06', d:3, o:2, q:"One job only “sorts 5,000 inquiries a day into categories.” Which choice is most reasonable in terms of cost, speed and quality?",
  c:["Process everything with the top model to maximize classification accuracy, and skip any human checking", "Use a light model and verify accuracy on samples; send only the hard cases to a stronger model or a person", "Have people classify everything, and use Claude only to tally the results and turn them into charts", "Do not classify at all; hand inquiries to staff in the order received and let them deal with each one"],
  a:[1], e:"Light models suit simple, high-volume tasks. Checking accuracy on samples and escalating only hard items is the cost-effective design. The official sample questions reason the same way." },
{ id:'ccao-3-07', d:3, o:2, q:"Which of the following is NOT an appropriate factor when deciding on a model?",
  c:["Task difficulty (depth of reasoning)", "Requirements for response speed", "Cost per item and the number of items", "How pleasant the model's name sounds"],
  a:[3], e:"The choice rests on task requirements: quality, speed and cost." },
{ id:'ccao-3-08', d:3, o:3, q:"After reading dozens of documents and a long exchange in one conversation, Claude has stopped following your original instructions. What is the best fix?",
  c:["Keep the conversation going and correct Claude on the spot only when an instruction is not followed", "Have it summarize the key points and decisions so far, then start a new conversation (or Project) with that", "Keep working, but add “please remember the original instructions” to every request that you send", "Paste all the original documents once more and restate the instructions at the end of the conversation"],
  a:[1], e:"The context window has a limit, and in long conversations older information loses influence. Summarizing and restarting is the standard move. This maps to the objective on when to restart, summarize or persist." },
{ id:'ccao-3-09', d:3, o:3, q:"Restating the same background (company name, your role, writing style) in every conversation is tedious. What is an appropriate solution?",
  c:["Paste it by hand each time; the background is short, so the effort is not a real problem", "Put it somewhere that persists across conversations, such as Project instructions or memory", "Leave it out and let Claude guess, correcting it on the spot only when it gets something wrong", "Merge everything into one conversation and keep making all requests there to keep the background"],
  a:[1], e:"Background that spans conversations belongs in Project instructions (or memory). Running one conversation forever hits the context limit." },
{ id:'ccao-3-10', d:3, o:3, q:"Which statement about the context window is correct?",
  c:["There is a limit to how much Claude can refer to in one exchange (history, attachments, instructions)", "Claude automatically remembers every past conversation forever and can refer to any of them", "Attachments are not part of the context; only the characters typed in the chat count toward it", "On reaching the limit, Claude automatically creates a new conversation and carries the key points over"],
  a:[0], e:"Context is limited, and history, attachments and instructions all sit inside it. Manage it by summarizing or splitting conversations." },
{ id:'ccao-3-11', d:3, o:0, q:"You want several colleagues to use an “internal policy Q&A” with the same setup. Which method fits best?",
  c:["Each person pastes the policy every time and asks in their own words", "Put the policy and answering rules in a Project and share it with the team", "Each person crafts their own prompts and shares the good ones later", "Paste the policy into an Artifact and copy from it whenever it is needed"],
  a:[1], e:"A Project bundles instructions and files into a shared workspace, so a team keeps the same setup." },

// ---- Domain 4: Workflow Integration and Solution Design (16%) ----
{ id:'ccao-4-01', d:4, o:0, q:"Your company is considering a new expense-reporting tool. What is the most effective way to use Claude for requirements analysis?",
  c:["Give it current steps, pain points and interview notes; have it organize candidate requirements, gaps and priorities. People decide", "Ask Claude to “decide which expense-reporting tool is best for our company” and adopt its answer exactly as it comes back", "Requirements analysis needs stakeholder agreement, so do not use Claude at all and let people settle everything in meetings", "Have Claude write generic expense-reporting requirements from scratch and circulate them to stakeholders as your own"],
  a:[0], e:"In requirements analysis Claude's role is to organize the information given, point out gaps and surface issues. Deciding is a human responsibility." },
{ id:'ccao-4-02', d:4, o:0, q:"Before a meeting to identify candidate use cases, which preparation with Claude is appropriate?",
  c:["Give it the department's task list and have it pull out tasks that are repetitive, document-centered and have clear criteria", "Have Claude run the meeting in your place, summarize what attendees say on the spot and decide on the candidates", "Thinking up candidates is human work, so do not use Claude and have attendees come up with them from scratch", "Have Claude produce only the meeting minutes, while the actual identification of candidates is done by people"],
  a:[0], e:"Claude is good at document-based, routine tasks with clear criteria. Extracting and organizing candidates is useful meeting preparation." },
{ id:'ccao-4-03', d:4, o:1, q:"You want to cut the monthly “industry trends report” from two days to half a day. Which design for using Claude is most appropriate?",
  c:["Have Claude write the whole report, tidy only the layout and submit it as it is to get down to half a day", "Split the work: Claude gathers, extracts key points and drafts; people check sources, interpret and decide", "Do not use Claude; revise the gathering steps and the division of duties so that people can work faster", "Have Claude automate everything from gathering to submission; people only confirm a completion notice"],
  a:[1], e:"When using Claude for research, planning and process optimization, separate what Claude does fast (gathering, organizing, drafting) from what people must own (verifying, judging)." },
{ id:'ccao-4-04', d:4, o:1, q:"Which TWO are highly effective things to ask Claude for when drawing up a project plan?",
  c:["Listing tasks and sorting out dependencies", "Pointing out risks and things overlooked", "Approving and deciding the final budget", "Formal sign-off and official notification"],
  a:[0,1], e:"Claude is good at structuring a plan and spotting gaps. Approval and sign-off are human responsibilities and are not things to hand to Claude." },
{ id:'ccao-4-05', d:4, o:2, q:"You are designing a new customer-response template. Which is an appropriate way to use Claude for design, development and iteration?",
  c:["Have it create a first version, test it on real inquiries to find weak spots, and revise repeatedly", "Treat the first version as final and run it without changes even when frontline staff give feedback", "Skip measuring how well the template works; distribute it and leave its use up to each person", "Have Claude reply to customers directly with the template and skip the check by the person in charge"],
  a:[0], e:"A solution is not finished when it is built; test it on real data and improve it iteratively." },
{ id:'ccao-4-06', d:4, o:2, q:"Which of the following is NOT appropriate to do before putting a Claude-based solution (for example, automatic FAQ drafts) into full operation?",
  c:["Run a pilot on real inquiries and measure quality", "Decide how people step in when it fails", "Skip the pilot and roll it out company-wide at once", "Decide who is to check the outputs"],
  a:[2], e:"A simultaneous rollout carries a lot of risk. Design the pilot, the intervention steps and the responsibility for checking first." },
{ id:'ccao-4-07', d:4, o:3, q:"You are bringing Claude into the existing inquiry-handling workflow. What is the most realistic first step?",
  c:["Replace every step with Claude and have staff simply receive a report of the results", "Add it to one step a person checks before use, such as drafting replies, and measure effects and problems", "Throw out the existing procedure, rebuild it around Claude from scratch and switch over all at once", "Introduce Claude behind the scenes without telling staff, and announce it formally once it shows results"],
  a:[1], e:"The standard approach is to start by augmenting the existing workflow and widen the scope as you measure results. Full replacement or secret adoption tends to fail." },
{ id:'ccao-4-08', d:4, o:3, q:"What is appropriate input for deciding whether to augment a workflow or redesign it?",
  c:["Where the bottleneck is in the current steps, how many steps Claude can take on, and the risk of the change", "Whether it is popular among competitors, and whether executives happen to be interested in the initiative", "The name of the Claude model you plan to use and where that model ranks on published benchmarks", "The age and years of service of the staff, and how strongly they resist adopting any new tools"],
  a:[0], e:"Augment or redesign is decided by bottlenecks, scope of application and risk." },
{ id:'ccao-4-09', d:4, o:4, q:"What is the most appropriate way to explain “the impact of adopting Claude” to executives?",
  c:["Raise big expectations that “it can do anything,” skip the details and get the adoption approved quickly", "Show concrete use cases, numbers such as hours saved, and limits (verification needed, sensitive data)", "Leave out limits and cautions; describe only strengths and other companies' successes for a positive mood", "Explain the mechanics in detail with technical terms so they fully understand before they decide"],
  a:[1], e:"Showing value and limits concretely sets the right expectations and governance. Inflated expectations cause failures." },
{ id:'ccao-4-10', d:4, o:4, q:"A frontline colleague opposes adoption, saying “AI can't be trusted.” What is an appropriate response for an associate?",
  c:["Objections delay adoption, so do not engage with them and go ahead exactly as planned", "Ask for the specific concerns, show the verification steps and human final check, and trial a small task together", "Persuade them by explaining that “it is the latest AI, so it is accurate and does not make mistakes”", "Tell them it is an executive directive to be followed, and that objecting will not change anything"],
  a:[1], e:"Acknowledging limits, building verification and human involvement into the design, and trying it small is what builds trust." },
{ id:'ccao-4-11', d:4, o:1, q:"What is the most useful information to give Claude for finding process improvements?",
  c:["The current procedure, time taken, and a record of common failures", "Words of praise and high expectations for Claude's performance", "Only a list of staff names and the departments they belong to", "The premise that the current process needs no improvement"],
  a:[0], e:"The more concrete the information about the current state, the better the bottleneck analysis and the improvement ideas." },

// ---- Domain 5: Configuration and Knowledge Management (12%) ----
{ id:'ccao-5-01', d:5, o:0, q:"What is the most appropriate content for a Project's instructions (custom instructions)?",
  c:["The role, purpose, style, output format and prohibitions that should always hold in that Project", "A one-time task you want done that day, together with its deadline and where it is to be submitted", "Highly confidential personal data of customers, plus a list of the materials you want made from it", "Nothing at all; say what is needed in each conversation and adapt it to the situation at that moment"],
  a:[0], e:"Project instructions are where you put standing assumptions that apply to every conversation in that Project. One-time tasks are requested in the conversation." },
{ id:'ccao-5-02', d:5, o:0, q:"Which TWO are appropriate materials to place in a Project's knowledge?",
  c:["Internal guidelines and glossaries you refer to often", "Good past examples you want answers to draw on", "Temporary notes and drafts that are used only today", "Customer data that policy forbids you to upload"],
  a:[0,1], e:"Knowledge is for stable material referred to repeatedly. Temporary notes belong in the conversation, and prohibited data is never placed there." },
{ id:'ccao-5-03', d:5, o:0, q:"After loading a large amount of material into a Project's knowledge, answers became off-target. What is the likely cause and fix?",
  c:["Loosely related files dilute the context. Narrow to files that fit the purpose, and state reference priority in the instructions", "More material means more support for answers, so add further files that seem related and see how things develop", "It is a defect in the knowledge feature, so stop using it and paste the material you need into every conversation", "A stronger model reads material better, so leave the files exactly as they are and simply switch to a higher model"],
  a:[0], e:"For knowledge, fit matters more than volume. Loosely related material is noise. Narrowing down and giving reference instructions improves it." },
{ id:'ccao-5-04', d:5, o:1, q:"When connecting a connector such as Google Drive to Claude, what should an associate confirm first?",
  c:["What the connection lets Claude access, and whether the organization's AI policy permits it", "Whether connecting means every file in Drive will be handled correctly and automatically", "How much free space the connected Drive has and the total number of files stored in it", "Where the name “connector” comes from and what other kinds of connectors are on offer"],
  a:[0], e:"A connector grants access to data. Confirm the scope and policy compliance first." },
{ id:'ccao-5-05', d:5, o:1, q:"An old version of a policy was left in the uploaded knowledge, and Claude answered from the old content. What is appropriate to prevent a recurrence?",
  c:["Delete the old version, mark files with version and date, and assign an owner and update frequency", "Leave the old version in place and add “look at the newer policy” every time you ask Claude", "Version control is hard in the knowledge feature, so stop using it and paste the policy each time", "Have a person reread the full policy for every answer and compare it with what Claude wrote"],
  a:[0], e:"The heart of knowledge management is keeping it current. Mark versions and name who is responsible for updates." },
{ id:'ccao-5-06', d:5, o:2, q:"Which is an effective way to write system-level instructions (such as Project instructions)?",
  c:["List abstract qualities like “polite,” “smart” and “accurate,” and leave the rest to Claude's own judgment", "State the role, readers, output format, prohibitions and behavior when unsure (ask; do not guess) concretely", "Make them as long as possible, dozens of pages covering every case, so that no exception can ever occur", "Write no instructions; ask on the spot in each conversation and say what is needed for flexibility"],
  a:[1], e:"Effective instructions are concrete and checkable. Abstract words alone do not change behavior, and instructions that are too long create contradictions and bury what matters." },
{ id:'ccao-5-07', d:5, o:2, q:"What is the aim of writing “when unsure, do not guess; say so and ask for confirmation” in the instructions?",
  c:["Reducing hallucination and making clear where people need to verify", "Making outputs longer so that readers get a courteous impression", "Making Claude respond more cautiously so that processing slows", "Tidying up the instructions so that the Project looks better"],
  a:[0], e:"Asking for uncertainty to be stated reduces unsupported assertions and shows people where to check." },
{ id:'ccao-5-08', d:5, o:3, q:"As you keep operating a Project, which practice is appropriate for maintaining and updating instructions and knowledge?",
  c:["Never change the instructions and knowledge once set; keep using the original design for the sake of stability", "Revise instructions based on output problems, update knowledge as documents change, and keep a change log", "Delete the whole Project and rebuild it every time a problem occurs, without carrying over past settings", "Leave updates to Claude and let it automatically fix instructions and knowledge whenever it judges it necessary"],
  a:[1], e:"Configuration is developed through operation: problems lead to revised instructions, document changes lead to updated knowledge, and both are recorded." },
{ id:'ccao-5-09', d:5, o:3, q:"Someone rewrote the instructions of a shared Project without telling anyone, and answer quality dropped. What is appropriate to prevent a recurrence?",
  c:["Record who changed the instructions and why, and assign an owner for changes plus a review step", "Remove the Project from everyone and have only those who need it rebuild and use it individually", "Empty the instructions, keep only the knowledge files and let each person manage in their own chats", "Have everyone create a separate Project so that nobody else is able to rewrite their instructions"],
  a:[0], e:"Change control for configuration (who, why, when) is basic for any shared resource." },

// ---- Domain 6: Governance, Risk, and Responsible Use (15%) ----
{ id:'ccao-6-01', d:6, o:0, q:"Which of the following is an INAPPROPRIATE use of Claude?",
  c:["Having Claude draft a manual for internal use", "Deciding final performance ratings from Claude's output alone", "Having Claude summarize meeting notes into a readable form", "Brainstorming a presentation outline with Claude for ideas"],
  a:[1], e:"Handing a high-impact judgment such as evaluating or rewarding people entirely to AI is inappropriate. Use it as support; judgment and accountability stay with people." },
{ id:'ccao-6-02', d:6, o:0, q:"Which TWO are characteristics of use cases that suit Claude?",
  c:["The work centers on documents and text", "A person can check whether the output is right", "An error at once causes an irreversible result", "It needs real-time control of physical equipment"],
  a:[0,1], e:"Text-centered, verifiable work is a good fit. Irreversible high-risk decisions and physical control are not." },
{ id:'ccao-6-03', d:6, o:1, q:"A marketer wants to upload a table containing customer names, emails and purchase history to Claude for trend analysis. Company policy restricts sharing personal data externally. What is the most appropriate action?",
  c:["The analysis is for internal purposes, so upload the table as it is and have the trends analyzed", "Remove or pseudonymize identifiers such as names and emails before uploading, then analyze within policy", "Upload the table as it is and instruct Claude: “Please do not remember any personal information”", "A table that contains personal information cannot be handled, so give up on the trend analysis itself"],
  a:[1], e:"This has the same shape as an official sample question. Removing identifiers or anonymizing lets you meet the goal while following policy. “Do not remember” is not a control." },
{ id:'ccao-6-04', d:6, o:1, q:"When handling data that falls under overseas regulation (such as GDPR), what should an associate do first?",
  c:["Regulation is for specialists, so do not worry and carry on working as usual", "Check with legal and information security, and follow the organization's policy", "Ask Claude what the regulation says and leave the legal judgment to Claude", "Avoid the company account and process the data in a personal account instead"],
  a:[1], e:"Regulatory matters follow the organization's policy and the judgment of specialist departments. Processing in a personal account is outside controls and dangerous." },
{ id:'ccao-6-05', d:6, o:2, q:"Your organization's AI policy says that “AI use must be disclosed on generated work.” What should the associate do?",
  c:["Disclosure lowers trust in the deliverable, so submit it without the disclosure", "Follow the policy and disclose the use of AI in the way that is prescribed", "Disclose only when a manager or recipient asks, and otherwise leave it out", "The policy is only guidance, so each person may decide whether to disclose"],
  a:[1], e:"The objective is to follow organizational policy and governance standards. Transparency is a pillar of responsible use." },
{ id:'ccao-6-06', d:6, o:2, q:"What is the problem with using an AI tool the company has not approved on business data because it is convenient?",
  c:["No problem. Using a handy tool to speed up work is exactly the kind of initiative that should be valued", "Data handling and contract terms fall outside the organization's control, risking leaks and compliance breaches", "It may only perform worse than approved tools; using it with business data is not a problem in itself", "The fee merely cannot be expensed and falls on you personally; the handling of business data is no issue"],
  a:[1], e:"This is so-called shadow AI. With tools outside controls, the terms for contracts, retention and training use are unknown, which is a risk to the organization." },
{ id:'ccao-6-07', d:6, o:3, q:"When reviewing a job advertisement written with Claude, what especially needs checking from an ethical standpoint?",
  c:["Whether there are discriminatory expressions or hidden assumptions about gender, age or nationality", "Whether the text exceeds the character limit or has so many paragraphs that it becomes hard to read", "Whether there are too many emoji, and whether the way emoji are used suits the medium it runs in", "Whether the typeface and font size comply with the company's internal design rules for documents"],
  a:[0], e:"AI output can carry skew from its training data. Checking for fairness and discrimination is basic to ethical use." },
{ id:'ccao-6-08', d:6, o:3, q:"Which statement about accountability in the use of AI is correct?",
  c:["Responsibility for AI-written text lies with the AI that generated it", "Whoever decides or publishes using the output is responsible", "Nobody at all bears responsibility for what an AI happens to output", "The vendor that provides the AI bears all of the responsibility"],
  a:[1], e:"AI is a tool, and the people and organization that adopt its output are responsible. That is exactly why verification and records are needed." },
{ id:'ccao-6-09', d:6, o:1, q:"Which TWO should you confirm before giving Claude material that contains confidential internal information?",
  c:["Whether the organization's policy permits AI use for that class of information", "What the data-handling terms are for the plan in use (personal or organizational)", "Whether the page count and total size of the attachments are within the limits", "Whether the fonts and formatting of the material are easy for Claude to read"],
  a:[0,1], e:"The information classification and the plan's data-handling terms (whether data is used for training, retention period and so on) must be confirmed before passing confidential information." },
{ id:'ccao-6-10', d:6, o:0, q:"For customer complaints, someone proposes automatically sending the replies Claude writes, exactly as written. What is an appropriate opinion for an associate?",
  c:["It is efficient and reduces the load on staff, so support the proposal and start automatic sending right away", "Messages to customers have high impact and risk errors or poor wording, so the process should include a human check", "Claude writes courteous text and never gets wording wrong, so making the sending automatic poses no problem", "Replying to complaints tends to make things worse, so rather than auto-sending, do not reply and wait and see"],
  a:[1], e:"Outbound messages, especially in emotional situations, need a human check. The objective is telling appropriate from inappropriate use cases." },

// ---- Domain 7: Troubleshooting and Optimization (10%) ----
{ id:'ccao-7-01', d:7, o:0, q:"Claude's output never comes out in the format you specified (a table). Which cause should you suspect first?",
  c:["The format instruction is vague or buried. Show a concrete example and make the instruction clear", "Claude has no ability to make tables, so the format instruction has to be changed to bullets", "A network problem is keeping part of the instruction from arriving, so check and resend it", "A browser display problem is breaking the table, so open it in another browser to check"],
  a:[0], e:"Most format drift comes from vague instructions and is fixed by an example and clarification. Claude can make tables." },
{ id:'ccao-7-02', d:7, o:0, q:"Answers inside a Project have become more generic and less grounded in the files than before. Which order of diagnosis is appropriate?",
  c:["Check whether the knowledge files or instructions were changed, then review how requests are worded", "Switch to a higher model at once and see whether answers become grounded in the files again", "Delete the Project, create a new one and load the files and instructions again from the start", "Conclude that Claude's performance has dropped, stop using it for a while and see what happens"],
  a:[0], e:"Checking for changes in configuration (files, instructions) first is the basic way to isolate the cause." },
{ id:'ccao-7-03', d:7, o:0, q:"Claude's answer was cut off partway. What is the most appropriate fix?",
  c:["Ask it to “continue,” or split the request into parts", "Repeat the same request until it comes out uncut", "Settle for the short answer and write the rest yourself", "Give up on it as a defect and work some other way"],
  a:[0], e:"Output length has a limit. Ask for the continuation or split the request." },
{ id:'ccao-7-04', d:7, o:1, q:"A colleague gives feedback that “Claude's summaries tend to drop numbers.” Which adjustment of approach is appropriate?",
  c:["Add “always keep figures, dates and proper nouns” to the instructions, and have a list of figures appended for easy checking", "The feedback is one person's impression, so do not act on it and continue summarizing with the same instructions as before", "Dropped numbers are a limit of summarizing as a task, so stop summarizing altogether and distribute the original text", "Change nothing in the instructions; after each summary, pick the numbers out of the source yourself and add them by hand"],
  a:[0], e:"Reflect feedback in improved instructions, and shape the output so it is easy to verify." },
{ id:'ccao-7-05', d:7, o:1, q:"What is an appropriate way to confirm that a revised prompt has really improved?",
  c:["Compare before-and-after outputs on the same inputs (a few samples) against set criteria", "Try the revised prompt once on a new input and adopt it if the result seems good", "Judge by the impression from reading both outputs and adopt whichever feels better", "Discard the earlier outputs, skip the comparison and judge the new output alone"],
  a:[0], e:"Compare on the same inputs and evaluate against criteria. Do not decide from one impression." },
{ id:'ccao-7-06', d:7, o:2, q:"Every day you give Claude the same five instructions in sequence. What is the most appropriate way to make this efficient?",
  c:["Put the steps into Project instructions and the reference files into knowledge, so that one request is enough", "Keep typing five times a day, but save the wording in a notepad to cut only the time spent on copying it", "Memorize the instructions, type shortened versions and count on Claude to work out what you really mean", "All five instructions are needed, so regard efficiency as unnecessary and carry on with the same routine"],
  a:[0], e:"Putting a routine workflow into Project instructions and knowledge improves both repeatability and efficiency." },
{ id:'ccao-7-07', d:7, o:2, q:"Which TWO help lower the cost of using Claude (time, fees) while keeping quality?",
  c:["Send simple work to a light model and only hard work to a higher one", "Tighten the prompt so fewer retries are needed and one pass is enough", "Always use the top model so that no quality problems ever come up", "Never end a conversation and keep doing all of the work in the same one"],
  a:[0,1], e:"Matching models to tasks and cutting rework through prompt quality are the basics of efficiency." },
{ id:'ccao-7-08', d:7, o:0, q:"Asked about a document, Claude replies “this is not stated in the material,” but the material does state it. What is a likely cause?",
  c:["The material is long and the passage is hard to find, or it was not read correctly (an image-only PDF, for example)", "Claude is unable to read attached material at all and always answers from its training-data knowledge alone", "The question was phrased too politely, so Claude could not connect its intent with the content of the material", "There is a cap on how often Claude may read files by weekday or time of day, and that cap had been exceeded"],
  a:[0], e:"A scanned-image PDF may yield no text. Pointing to the page or converting it to text resolves this." },
// ---- added 2026-11-18 ----
{ id:'ccao-1-14', d:1, o:0, q:"Which set correctly lists the five elements of a prompt?",
  c:["Role, Context, Task, Constraints and Output format", "Purpose, Reader, Length, Tone and Deadline", "Question, Example, Temperature, Model and Language", "Background, Request, Expectation, Thanks and Signature"],
  a:[0], e:"The biggest cause of generic output is missing context." },
{ id:'ccao-1-15', d:1, o:2, q:"You dislike the output and are about to rewrite the whole prompt. Which approach matches diagnostic iteration?",
  c:["Tie the complaint to whichever of the five elements is missing and fix only that point; a full rewrite loses what was working and hides what helped", "Rewrite everything and compare; partial fixes change too little to show a direction, so a complete rewrite converges on a good prompt much sooner", "Switch to a higher model and send the same prompt; this works more surely than editing and spares you from working out which element is missing", "Send the same prompt three times and choose the best output; using the natural variation raises quality without having to fix the prompt at all"],
  a:[0], e:"When the gains get small, stop iterating and finish by hand." },
{ id:'ccao-1-16', d:1, o:3, q:"Which way of building a prompt is recommended for a brainstorming request?",
  c:["Give the goal and a minimal frame, and ask for volume and range; criteria and scope as strict as in analysis narrow the thinking", "Specify criteria and scope as strictly as for analysis; without a frame, off-target ideas multiply and choosing takes more effort", "Fix the output format as a table and limit the number of ideas to three; too many cannot be chosen from, so narrow from the start", "Set the role to “critic” and have flaws written for each idea; once flaws are known only good ideas remain, so range is not needed"],
  a:[0], e:"Tighten and loosen different things by task type: criteria and scope for analysis; questions and sources for research; reader, tone and format for drafting." },
{ id:'ccao-2-19', d:2, o:0, q:"Which are the three standards to hold Claude's output against when evaluating it?",
  c:["The requirements you set, the source material you supplied and the professional standards of the field; not how polished it looks", "Length of the text, readability and absence of typos; if the form is tidy the content can be trusted and requirement checks skipped", "Response speed, token count and model tier; judge whether it was worth the cost, and evaluate the content only after the cost", "A confident tone, specific figures and the number of sources; the more specific it is the more accurate, so firm statements can be trusted"],
  a:[0], e:"Accuracy and completeness are different things. Omissions do not catch the eye, so check for them separately." },
{ id:'ccao-2-20', d:2, o:1, q:"Which of the following counts as a typical hallucination?",
  c:["Claiming to have performed an action it did not perform, such as “I have sent it”", "Answering “I don't know” and asking the user to confirm, without offering a guess", "Returning only a summary with no source URL and leaving the details to the user", "Replying to a question with a clarifying question and holding back the answer itself"],
  a:[0], e:"Others: unsupported claims, fabricated overly specific figures or sources, firm statements unrelated to actual confidence, contradictions inside long text, and skew toward a leading question." },
{ id:'ccao-2-21', d:2, o:2, q:"You want to confirm that the figures in an important report are right. Which position is correct?",
  c:["Having Claude confirm itself is not independent verification. Check against source material or authoritative outside sources, and run calculations with code execution", "If you ask Claude to “check that this is really right,” it will point out any errors. That counts as independent verification, so no outside check is needed at all", "Send the same question twice; if the answers match, they are right. With no variation it can be trusted, and there is no need to check sources or outside information", "Ask a higher model the same question; if the answers agree, they are right. A different model makes it independent verification, and code execution is not needed"],
  a:[0], e:"Grounding techniques: restrict to the supplied material, ask for the quoted passages, have it extract first and analyze second, and run more than once to look for disagreements." },
{ id:'ccao-2-22', d:2, o:3, q:"Which are the four thresholds for deciding whether human review is mandatory?",
  c:["Stakes (size of impact), Reversibility (can it be undone), Audience (who will read it) and Regulatory exposure", "Length, Deadline, Seniority of the author and Model used; long documents and top-model output always get a human look", "Sources, Numbers, Language and Format; any output with figures and any output in English always gets a human look", "Cost, Speed, Quality and user Satisfaction; costly output and output with low satisfaction always get a human look"],
  a:[0], e:"Decide in advance that customer-facing final deliverables, finance and audit calculations, regulated data and public documents never go out without human review." },
{ id:'ccao-3-12', d:3, o:0, q:"Which correctly describes the division of roles among the feature layers?",
  c:["Projects hold context, Skills define procedures, code execution verifies calculations, memory keeps continuity. Each is independent", "Projects define procedures, Skills hold context, memory verifies calculations, code execution keeps continuity. Each is independent", "The four form a hierarchy: without a Project you cannot use Skills, memory or code execution, so create a Project first of all", "The four are different names for the same feature; using any one makes the rest unnecessary, so a Project alone covers everything"],
  a:[0], e:"Test for creating a Project: at least two of recurring work, shared background, and a consistent output format." },
{ id:'ccao-3-13', d:3, o:3, q:"In a long conversation, the early instructions have stopped working. Which gives the three remedies and what NOT to do?",
  c:["Restart, Summarize and carry over, or Persist in a Project or memory. Repeating the instructions or upgrading the model does not work", "Send the same instructions again, switch to a higher model, lower the temperature. Splitting the chat loses context, so recover within the same one", "Delete the conversation, erase all memory, switch to incognito. Leftover information is in the way, so wiping every memory restores the instructions", "Put the instructions in bold, write them in English, move them to the end. Emphasis makes them work, so splitting or summarizing is unnecessary"],
  a:[0], e:"Context is a budget." },
{ id:'ccao-4-12', d:4, o:3, q:"Drafting went well, so you want to let an agent send messages to customers too. What is the right assessment?",
  c:["Good drafting is no reason to hand over judgment. Irreversible steps such as approving, signing and sending stay with people; this is over-delegation", "If the previous step went well, the next can be handed over too. A track record is the basis of trust, and simple steps like sending are the easiest", "It can be handed over if Claude checks “is there any problem?” before sending. The self-check stands in for review, so no human approval is needed", "Hand over sending as well and take it back if complaints come. Trying small and learning is the basis of delegation, even for irreversible steps"],
  a:[0], e:"Delegation mapping: use reversibility, stakes, the need for human judgment or empathy, and accountability to sort steps into AI executes, human keeps, or shared." },
{ id:'ccao-4-13', d:4, o:4, q:"In a briefing for executives you are about to write “Claude will handle this work.” How should it be revised?",
  c:["State what it can and cannot do without overstating or understating, and name the human review steps that remain. “Fully automatic” is an overstatement", "Strengthen it to “Claude will handle this fully automatically.” Executives need plain wording, and the review steps are nothing more than a detail", "Leave the limits out. Writing limits stalls adoption, so list only what it can do and explain case by case when a problem actually comes up", "Blur it to “AI will handle this.” Naming the product invites questions of responsibility, so keep the actor vague and omit the review steps as well"],
  a:[0], e:"When other departments come to depend on the setup day to day, that is the signal to hand it over to a Developer or Architect." },
{ id:'ccao-5-10', d:5, o:0, q:"Which is the most common configuration mistake in a Project?",
  c:["Putting things in the wrong place, such as procedures in the instructions or rules in knowledge. Rules go in standing instructions, reference files in knowledge, procedures in Skills, decisions in memory", "Too much knowledge. More files make output slower, so narrow the files down to one and keep the procedures and rules inside that same file as well, so that everything is found in a single place", "Instructions that are too short. Longer instructions are followed better, so write the procedures, the content of the reference files and every decision into the instruction text itself", "Creating Skills per Project. A Skill can only be used inside one Project, so the same procedure has to be rebuilt once for every Project you have, however many of them there are"],
  a:[0], e:"Skills belong to the account and can be used from any Project. Configure a rule together with the material that rule refers to." },
{ id:'ccao-5-11', d:5, o:1, q:"With the Gmail connector you asked Claude to “send an email,” and it failed. Which explanation is correct?",
  c:["Each connector has a fixed range of actions for its service (for example, search and read but not send). Failing outside that range is not a defect", "It is a defect, so reconnect. After reconnecting, sending will work too; whether the organization approved it has no bearing on the range of actions", "The prompt is badly written. Writing “you must send it” firmly makes it send, so there is no need to look into the connector's range of actions", "Switch to a higher model. Model capability widens what a connector can do, so actions like sending are available only on the higher models"],
  a:[0], e:"Use connectors your organization has approved. Choose between uploads and connectors by freshness and maintenance effort." },
{ id:'ccao-5-12', d:5, o:3, q:"“The output used to be good, but lately it has drifted.” What is the right diagnosis?",
  c:["Suspect configuration decay. No error appears; quality just falls quietly. Isolate the layer: instructions, knowledge, Skill version or memory", "The model was updated, so rewrite the prompt. Leave configuration alone and overhaul the prompt wording to suit the new model", "The prompt has aged, so rewrite all of it. Configuration is unrelated; to get the old output back, throw the old prompt away", "Switch to a higher model. Capability solves falling quality, so upgrade the model before inspecting configuration or prompts"],
  a:[0], e:"“It used to work” is the signal to suspect configuration decay." },
{ id:'ccao-6-13', d:6, o:0, q:"A colleague recommends a Skill of unknown origin. Which response fits a proper trust assessment of a Skill?",
  c:["A Skill is software. Check its provider, what it can reach and whether that is excessive for its purpose, then enable it, refer it to an admin or reject it. Do not enable it on your own judgment alone", "If a colleague already uses it, it is safe. Enable it as it is; there is no need to spend effort checking the provider or what it can reach, because the colleague would have noticed any problem", "A Skill requests no permissions, so enabling it does no harm. It runs independently of the access your session has, which means there is no reason to be concerned about where it came from", "If it was made in-house it is already verified, so no check is needed. Confirm the provider only for outside Skills, and there is no need to consider whether it is excessive for the purpose"],
  a:[0], e:"A Skill requests no permissions; it inherits whatever access the session it runs in has. In-house does not automatically mean verified." },
{ id:'ccao-6-14', d:6, o:1, q:"You want to analyze survey results that include customers' personal information. Which procedure is right?",
  c:["Classify before input, and anonymize or mask before uploading. Watch for leftovers in free-text fields. When in doubt, treat it as the stricter class", "Upload first, then ask Claude to “anonymize the personal information.” Claude finds it more completely and processes it more reliably than by hand", "Analyze in incognito mode. If nothing stays in memory it may be entered, and data-retention policy does not apply while incognito is on", "Just delete the name column. Free-text fields hold no personal information, so removing the column completes anonymization with no classifying"],
  a:[0], e:"Incognito keeps things out of memory; whether data may be entered is a separate question. The organization's data-retention policy still applies." },
{ id:'ccao-6-15', d:6, o:2, q:"The approved procedure is cumbersome, and the team is drifting to unapproved tools. Which corrective measure is right?",
  c:["Make the right path easy to use, and tell apart whether the drift comes from a flawed rule or from a lack of training and awareness", "Ban the unapproved tools and record who violates the ban; rules are not kept without penalties, so tighter monitoring comes first", "Change the unapproved tools to approved; whatever people actually use is the right answer, so bring the policy in line with practice", "Delete the policy; a rule that is not kept is the same as no rule at all, so cut down on rules and give people freedom"],
  a:[0], e:"Policy compliance is a habit kept up through small everyday decisions." },
{ id:'ccao-7-09', d:7, o:0, q:"Which mapping of symptom → cause is correct?",
  c:["Off-target from the first reply → under-specified / stops complying midway → context overload / figures slightly off → use code execution / used to be good → configuration decay", "Off-target from the first reply → configuration decay / stops complying midway → under-specified / figures off → upgrade the model / used to be good → context overload", "Every symptom → rewrite the prompt. No isolating of causes is needed; whatever the symptom, rewriting all five of the elements will fix the problem", "Every symptom → upgrade the model. Symptoms disappear on a higher model, so switch models before spending any time isolating the cause"],
  a:[0], e:"In the new guide (from December 7), isolating causes is spread across Domains 1 and 5, and efficiency moves to Domain 4." },
{ id:'ccao-7-10', d:7, o:2, q:"You make the same correction to the output every single time. What is recommended?",
  c:["Promote the correction into a standing instruction or a Skill. If results vary by person, unify with shared Skills and knowledge", "Keep correcting by hand. Changing configuration affects other outputs, so correcting each time is safer and gives the same result", "Start a new conversation for each correction. Long conversations degrade, so beginning fresh every time reduces corrections", "Switch to a higher model. Needing corrections means the model lacks capability, so configuration or Skills will not fix it"],
  a:[0], e:"Design for reuse: background in Project knowledge, procedures in Skills, calculations with code execution." },
];
